.SH NAME
ktm-guide-preprocessor \- The preprocessor (chapter 25 of the ktm language guide)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The preprocessor runs before the compiler proper: it defines named values with \fB#define\fR, keeps or drops code with \fB#if\fR, pastes files in with \fB#include\fR, and links C libraries with \fB#link\fR\&. It is C's preprocessor, simplified: a macro names one value, and there are no function-like macros. This chapter covers \fB#define\fR, how a macro's value is evaluated, conditional compilation, macros from the command line, the predefined macros, \fB#include\fR and \fB#link\fR\&.
.SH #define
.PP
\fB#define NAME value\fR names a number, a string, or an expression of other macros. The name can then be used wherever its value could:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#define APP_NAME "demo"
#define VERSION 3
#define BUFFER (VERSION * 1024)
#define RATIO (VERSION / 2.0)

i32 main() {
    printf("%s v%d, %d bytes, %.1f\en", APP_NAME, VERSION, BUFFER, RATIO);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
demo v3, 3072 bytes, 1.5
.EE
.RE
.PP
A macro has the type of its value: \fBf64\fR if any operand is a float, otherwise \fBi64\fR\&. Comparisons and \fB&&\fR, \fB||\fR, \fB!\fR give an \fBi64\fR 0 or 1, and a cast to a built-in type sets the type:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#define HALF ((f64)5 / 2)
#define BIG (1 << 40)
#define OK (3 > 2 && !0)

i32 main() {
    printf("%.1f %d %d\en", HALF, BIG, OK);
    printf("%s %s\en", typeof(HALF), typeof(OK));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
2.5 1099511627776 1
f64 i64
.EE
.RE
.SS A macro is a value, not text
.PP
A macro's value is worked out once, as a whole. Using it inside a larger expression uses that value, so the parentheses C needs around a macro's body aren't needed:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#define TWO_PLUS_ONE 2 + 1

i32 main() {
    printf("%d\en", TWO_PLUS_ONE * 10);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
30
.EE
.RE
.PP
In C, the same macro would paste \fB2 + 1 * 10\fR and give 12.
.IP \(bu 2
Names in a macro's value must be other macros; variables, structs and functions are unknown to it.
.IP \(bu 2
A line ending in \fB\e\fR goes on to the next.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#define WIDTH 80
#define HEIGHT 24
#define CELLS WIDTH * \e
              HEIGHT

i32 main() {
    printf("%d\en", CELLS);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1920
.EE
.RE
.SS No function-like macros
.PP
A macro can't take parameters. \fB#define SQ(x) ((x) * (x))\fR is an error; write a function, which the compiler inlines:
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
#define SQ(x) ((x) * (x))

i32 main() {
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
function\-like macros are not supported
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i64 sq(i64 x) {
    return x * x;
}

i32 main() {
    printf("%d\en", sq(7));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
49
.EE
.RE
.PP
With a space before the parenthesis, \fB#define CALC (2 * 21)\fR is an ordinary macro.
.SS Macros or constants
.PP
For a number used in code, a \fBconst\fR is usually better: it has a declared type and follows the scope rules of variables (see Variables (see ktm-guide-variables(7))). A macro is what \fB#if\fR needs, and what a \fB\-D\fR from the command line sets.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
const i64 MAX_USERS = 100;
#define MAX_GROUPS 10

i32 main() {
    i64 slots[MAX_GROUPS];
    printf("%d %d\en", MAX_USERS, sizeof(slots) / sizeof(i64));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
100 10
.EE
.RE
.SS #undef
.PP
\fB#undef NAME\fR removes a macro from that point on; code above it keeps the old value:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#define MODE 1

i32 main() {
    printf("%d\en", MODE);
#undef MODE
#ifndef MODE
    printf("MODE is gone\en");
#endif
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1
MODE is gone
.EE
.RE
.SH Conditional compilation
.PP
\fB#if\fR, \fB#ifdef\fR, \fB#ifndef\fR, \fB#elif\fR, \fB#else\fR and \fB#endif\fR keep or drop lines, as in C. \fBdefined(NAME)\fR tests whether a macro exists:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#define LEVEL 2

i32 main() {
#if LEVEL > 2
    printf("high\en");
#elif LEVEL == 2 && !defined(QUIET)
    printf("level two\en");
#else
    printf("low\en");
#endif
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
level two
.EE
.RE
.PP
Dropped lines aren't compiled at all, so they may use names that don't exist:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i32 main() {
#ifdef FEATURE_X
    featureX();
#endif
    printf("built without FEATURE_X\en");
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
built without FEATURE_X
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fB#error message\fR stops the build with the message, for a configuration that can't work:
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
#define THREADS 0

#if THREADS < 1
#error "THREADS must be at least 1"
#endif

i32 main() {
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
THREADS must be at least 1
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
An \fB#else\fR or \fB#endif\fR with no \fB#if\fR, a second \fB#else\fR, or an \fB#if\fR never closed is an error.
.IP \(bu 2
Extra tokens after an \fB#if\fR condition are an error.
.SH From the command line
.PP
\fB\-DNAME\fR defines a macro, with no value, for the whole build:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i32 main() {
#ifdef VERBOSE
    printf("verbose mode\en");
#else
    printf("quiet mode\en");
#endif
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ktm \-DVERBOSE app.ktm \-o app && ./app
verbose mode
$ ktm app.ktm \-o app && ./app
quiet mode
.EE
.RE
.SH Predefined macros
.PP
.TS
allbox tab(	);
lb lb
l lx .
T{
Macro
T}	T{
Defined when
T}
T{
\fBIS_LINUX\fR, \fB__linux__\fR
T}	T{
compiling for Linux
T}
T{
\fBIS_MACOS\fR, \fB__macos__\fR
T}	T{
compiling for macOS
T}
T{
\fBIS_X86_64\fR, \fB__x86_64__\fR
T}	T{
targeting x86_64
T}
T{
\fBIS_ARM_64\fR, \fB__aarch64__\fR
T}	T{
targeting arm64
T}
T{
\fB__ARCH__\fR
T}	T{
always: the architecture's name, as a string
T}
T{
\fB__KTM_JIT__\fR / \fB__KTM_AOT__\fR
T}	T{
running in the JIT or the REPL / compiling a binary
T}
T{
\fB__KTM_VERSION__\fR, \fB__DATE__\fR, \fB__TIME__\fR, \fB__TIMESTAMP__\fR
T}	T{
always
T}
T{
\fB__KTM_LINK_SQLITE3__\fR
T}	T{
ktm was built with SQLite
T}
.TE
.PP
Code for one platform:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#ifdef IS_LINUX
u8 *os = "Linux";
#elif defined(IS_MACOS)
u8 *os = "macOS";
#else
#error "unsupported platform"
#endif
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i32 main() {
#if defined(IS_LINUX) || defined(IS_MACOS)
    printf("a supported system\en");
#endif
#if defined(IS_X86_64) || defined(IS_ARM_64)
    printf("a 64\-bit processor\en");
#endif
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
a supported system
a 64\-bit processor
.EE
.RE
.PP
To check another platform's branches without its machine, compile to assembly for it: \fBktm \-S \-\-target=aarch64\-apple\-darwin file.ktm\fR\&.
.SS #ifjit and #ifaot
.PP
\fB#ifjit\fR and \fB#ifaot\fR are short for \fB#ifdef __KTM_JIT__\fR and \fB#ifdef __KTM_AOT__\fR:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i32 main() {
#ifjit
    printf("running in the JIT\en");
#else
    printf("running as a compiled binary\en");
#endif
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ktm app.ktm \-o app && ./app
running as a compiled binary
$ ktm \-jit app.ktm
running in the JIT
.EE
.RE
.SH #include and #link
.PP
\fB#include "file"\fR pastes a file in, relative to the including file; \fB#include <file>\fR reads it from the installed include directory. For ktm code, prefer modules (see Modules and packages (see ktm-guide-modules-and-packages(7))): they are compiled once and keep their private names private.
.PP
\fB#link <name>\fR links a C library, like \fB\-lname\fR; \fB#link "path"\fR links a library file. It works for compiled programs, the JIT and the REPL alike (see Calling C (see ktm-guide-calling-c(7))):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#link <sqlite3>          // like \-lsqlite3
#link "./libmine.so"     // a path, relative to the working directory
.EE
.RE
.PP
A file that can't be read is an error at the \fB#include\fR, and the rest of the file is still checked.
.SH Rules
.IP \(bu 2
\fB#define NAME value\fR names one value: a number, a string, or an expression of macros. Its type is \fBf64\fR with any float operand, otherwise \fBi64\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
A macro's value is evaluated as a whole before it is used; there are no function-like macros.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB#if\fR/\fB#ifdef\fR/\fB#ifndef\fR/\fB#elif\fR/\fB#else\fR/\fB#endif\fR/\fB#undef\fR/\fB#error\fR work as in C; \fB#ifjit\fR/\fB#ifaot\fR test the JIT.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-DNAME\fR defines a macro from the command line.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB#include\fR pastes a file; \fB#link\fR links a C library.
